# =============================================================================
# Logging / Events
# =============================================================================
# User action events are buffered in Redis, forwarded by the OpenTelemetry
# collector and, by default, stored in the Loki that ships with this stack.
# The dashboard lives at http://127.0.0.1:3000 (folder Pneumatic).

# local = bundled Loki + Grafana, otlp / elasticsearch = external stack,
# none = pipeline off. Anything but `local` also needs a COMPOSE_FILE line,
# see the bottom of this section.
# LOGS_BACKEND=local
# LOGS_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318

# The file service writes into the same journal (uploads, downloads and
# refused downloads) and reads LOGS_BACKEND, REDIS_PASSWORD and
# LOGS_STREAM_MAXLEN from this file too; there is nothing to configure for it
# separately. Deploy order on an upgrade: backend first, then the file service.
# The backend declares the file event types; a file service ahead of it would
# have its records filed under the debug category with the file name exposed.

# --- Event buffer: db4 of the Redis above ---
# Events wait in a Redis stream between emit() and the collector, in db4 of
# the same Redis that holds the cache, the tokens, the channels and the
# sessions; REDIS_PASSWORD opens it, there is no separate password. The
# stream is the only copy of an event until the collector has it, and the
# Redis runs with RDB snapshots only (--save 20 1 ...): a crash costs what was
# written since the last snapshot, at most ~20 seconds of events. Accepted on
# 2026-09-09: one Redis to run instead of two.

# How many events the buffer keeps before the oldest are dropped. It is the
# memory budget of the stream, and the only bound on it - the Redis has no
# memory ceiling: worst case bytes = event size x MAXLEN.
# Measured, not guessed: an event serialises to 620 B on average
# (task.complete 721, workflow.run 622, user.login 518) and a Redis stream
# entry costs ~14 % on top, so ~700 B. 1 KB is the planning figure, it covers
# long workflow and task names.
# The default 250 000 therefore budgets 250 MB, and the dead letter stream
# adds 10 MB; both sit in the memory of the shared Redis next to the cache.
# What this buys: how long the collector may stay down without losing events.
# At 1 event/s that is ~69 hours, at 10/s ~7 hours, at 100/s ~42 minutes.
# Measure your own rate before changing it. Workflow events are the bulk of
# the journal and they are rows of the main database, so their rate per
# second over the last day is:
#   select count(*) / 86400.0 from processes_workflowevent
#   where created > now() - interval '1 day';
# Sign-ins, template edits, webhook changes and file downloads add to that
# and are not in the table.
# LOGS_STREAM_MAXLEN=250000
# LOGS_CONSUMER_BATCH_SIZE=1000

# --- Local stack: Loki + Grafana (LOGS_BACKEND=local) ---
# LOGS_RETENTION_PERIOD=365d
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin

# REQUIRED, no default. A Grafana account reads the audit journal of every
# account in the installation, so `docker compose` refuses to run until this is
# set to a password of your own. Fill the line in and uncomment it.
# Upgrading an installation whose .env predates this line: start.sh adds it
# to an existing .env; without start.sh add it by hand before
# `docker compose up`. The value is applied only when the
# pneumatic_grafana-data volume is created, so editing this line later
# changes nothing; the password of a running installation is changed with
#   docker exec pneumatic-grafana grafana cli admin reset-admin-password NEW
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# GRAFANA_BIND=127.0.0.1
# GRAFANA_PORT=3000

# The dashboard that opens on sign in. Any file of the provisioned directory
# /etc/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/json/ (the repository directory
# logging/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/json/) will do; the default is the
# overview of the whole installation.
# GRAFANA_HOME_DASHBOARD=/etc/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/json/project-overview.json

# Alert rules for failed sign-ins, refused file downloads and a pipeline that
# stopped delivering are provisioned from
# logging/grafana/provisioning/alerting/rules.yaml - thresholds are edited
# there, and Grafana re-reads the file on restart, not on a timer. They fire
# into the Grafana UI only until a contact point is added under
# Alerting -> Contact points.

# --- External OTLP receiver (LOGS_BACKEND=otlp) ---
# LOGS_OTLP_EXPORT_ENDPOINT=
# LOGS_OTLP_EXPORT_AUTH=

# --- Elasticsearch (LOGS_BACKEND=elasticsearch) ---
# The store itself - node, Kibana, certificates, roles, retention, snapshots -
# lives in logging/elasticsearch and runs on a machine of its own: the header
# of its docker-compose.yml lists the steps from an empty server to a working
# journal, and elasticsearch.env.example next to it every setting. What
# follows is only this side of the wire.
#
# This mode needs three lines in .env, not one:
#   LOGS_BACKEND=elasticsearch
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:logging/compose/logs-external.yml:logging/elasticsearch/docker-compose.collector-elasticsearch.yml
#   LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_URL=https://...
# The third compose file carries the disk for the sending queue and the
# variables below; without it the collector starts, delivers nothing and loses
# whatever it was holding on the next restart.

# Address of the store. https, always: this is the audit journal of every
# account in the installation, and the host name has to be one of the names
# the certificate was issued for (ES_CERT_DNS on the storage machine).
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_URL=https://es.pneumatic.internal:9200

# How the collector authenticates. An API key is the way: it carries a role
# that can append to the journal and nothing else, it expires on a date of its
# own, and rotating it is this one line plus a restart - see
# logging/elasticsearch/scripts/rotate-api-key.sh, which prints the value.
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_API_KEY=

# The login and password of an Elasticsearch account, as an alternative to the
# key above. Set one or the other, never both. A password is worse: it belongs
# to a person, it has no expiry date, and changing it takes the account away
# from everything else that used it.
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_USER=
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_PASSWORD=

# The certificate authority of the store, inside the collector container. The
# file is logging/elasticsearch/certs/ca/ca.crt on the storage machine; copy
# it to logging/otel/es-ca.crt here - the otel directory is already mounted
# read only, so no compose change is needed. An empty value means the system
# certificate store, for a store with a publicly trusted certificate.
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_CA_FILE=/etc/otel/es-ca.crt

# The data stream the events are written to. The name is not free-form:
# `logs-<dataset>.otel-<namespace>` is what Elasticsearch recognises as an
# OTel log stream, and it is what the index template, the retention policy and
# the writer role are bound to. Change it only together with ES_DATA_STREAM
# and ES_INDEX_PATTERN on the storage machine.
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_INDEX=logs-pneumatic.events.otel-default

# Where the collector keeps the queue of batches it has not delivered yet.
# The default is the mount point of the docker volume from the third compose
# file; there is no reason to change it.
# LOGS_ELASTICSEARCH_QUEUE_DIR=/var/lib/otelcol/queue

# --- Do not start the bundled Loki and Grafana ---
# Pick one line matching LOGS_BACKEND, otherwise the local stack keeps running.
# The `none` file also skips the collector. Set LOGS_BACKEND=none together
# with it - the override alone, with the pipeline still on, means events pile
# up in the buffer with nothing to drain it.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:logging/compose/logs-external.yml
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:logging/compose/logs-none.yml

# =============================================================================
# File Service
# =============================================================================
# FILE_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost/files
# FILE_DEBUG=false
# FILE_CONFIG=Production
# FILE_WORKERS=1
# FILE_PORT=8000
# FILE_FASTAPI_BASE_URL=http://localhost/files
# FILE_MAX_FILE_SIZE=104857600
# FILE_BUCKET_PREFIX=pneumatic-dev-test
# FILE_CHUNK_SIZE=1048576

# --- File Service Auth (internal, must match Django backend) ---
# FILE_KEY_PREFIX_REDIS=:1:
# FILE_AUTH_TOKEN_ITERATIONS=1

# --- File Service Database ---
# FILE_POSTGRES_USER=pneumatic
# FILE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pneumatic
# FILE_POSTGRES_DB=pneumatic
# FILE_POSTGRES_HOST=file-postgres
# FILE_POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- File Service Storage: Local (SeaweedFS) ---
# FILE_STORAGE_TYPE=local
# FILE_SEAWEEDFS_S3_ENDPOINT=http://seaweedfs-filer:8333
# FILE_SEAWEEDFS_S3_ACCESS_KEY=any-secret-key-will-work
# FILE_SEAWEEDFS_S3_SECRET_KEY=any-secret-key-will-work
# FILE_SEAWEEDFS_S3_REGION=us-central1
# FILE_SEAWEEDFS_S3_USE_SSL=false

# --- File Service Storage: Google Cloud ---
# To use GCS, set FILE_STORAGE_TYPE=google and fill in the values below.
# FILE_GCS_S3_ENDPOINT=
# FILE_GCS_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
# FILE_GCS_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# FILE_GCS_S3_REGION=


# =============================================================================
# Billing
# =============================================================================
